# answered by claude-opus-5-5, prompt /lib/phil/senses b350a3107fa4, 2026-10-19
sensible	one
perceived_by_sense	two	perceive	P2	perceived by sense in the ordinary way: the house or river itself is what is seen or touched	immediately perceived: what is directly present to the mind in sensing
idea	one
can_exist_unperceived	one
# The text treats "the things we perceive by sense" (P1) and what we "perceive besides our own ideas" (P2) as one notion.
# P1 is plausible only if "perceive" covers the external objects that perception is of. P2 is plausible only if "perceive" means immediate perception of what is present to the mind.
# A house could be perceived by sense in the first sense without being an idea. So the claim could be true as P1 uses it and false as P2 uses it.
# I name no bridge. The shift happens between P1 and P2 inside the one claim, not through a statement that joins two claims.
# I was unsure about "idea". It may also shift: P2 would need sensation as the object sensed, while P3 is most plausible for sensation as the act of sensing (Moore's act/object point). I left it as one sense.
